Horst was given to the Church as a child, not out of cruelty, but out of necessity. It was common enough—too many mouths, too few resources. The Church raised him, fed him, and gave him a place in the world. For that, it earned his loyalty.

Not his faith.

Horst never became a true believer. To him, the gods were distant at best, irrelevant at worst. What mattered was that the Church functioned. It brought order, contained chaos, and dealt with problems others couldn’t. That alone made it necessary.

Not righteous. Not pure. But necessary.

Growing up within its walls, Horst learned to see both sides. The public face of virtue—and the quieter reality beneath it. Corruption, buried reports, decisions made for convenience rather than truth. Over time, he came to understand the Church for what it was: a flawed institution holding worse things at bay.

He could live with that.

He found his role not in prayer, but in investigation. While others memorized scripture, Horst learned to observe, to question, to piece things together. Most cases turned out to be nothing—fear, superstition, or people desperate to be taken seriously.

The Church knew this.

So they sent him.

Not an Inquisitor. Never officially.

Horst became what some might call a “second-class fire extinguisher”—the kind you use when something isn’t important enough to acknowledge, or too inconvenient to make public. If people needed reassurance, he provided it. If something needed to disappear quietly, he handled it. His reports were written, filed, and often forgotten.

Truth was secondary.

Resolution was what mattered.

Horst approached his work with a dry, pragmatic mindset. The supernatural, if it existed, was simply another variable. Something to account for. Something to endure.

That belief didn’t survive intact.

Bored by the monotony of prayer, Horst once experimented with mushrooms grown in the monastery gardens. What followed was not a vision in the religious sense, but something far less comforting.

Something noticed him.

Or perhaps it always had—and he had finally made himself visible.

The entity did not introduce itself. It did not demand devotion. It simply was—pressing into his perception in ways that felt wrong, vast, and deeply impersonal. Shapes that did not resolve. Thoughts that were not his own. A presence that resembled less a voice and more an observation.

When it ended, Horst was not alone anymore.

And he was not unchanged.

He had gained power—but also something else: a constant pressure at the edge of his mind. Visions, dreams, fragments of something that did not align with human understanding.

Horst adapted.

He does not drink. Alcohol dulls the mind, lowers defenses, makes one vulnerable—and Horst has learned that vulnerability is not an option. Not anymore.

Instead, he relies on controlled measures. Herbs from the monastery gardens, carefully prepared and smoked. Not for pleasure, but for distance. For control. They do not silence what he experiences—but they help him endure it.

Because that is the key.

You do not understand something like this.

You endure it.

You build distance. Detachment. A kind of quiet indifference.

If you can reach that state—if you can truly stop caring, even for a moment—you can look at things no human mind was meant to process… and remain standing.

Horst continues his work as before.

He investigates. He resolves. He files his reports.

The Church still sends him where things are uncertain, inconvenient, or better left unexamined. They see a reliable tool—pragmatic, quiet, effective.

They do not ask what changed.

Horst does not offer.

Because at this point, the distinction hardly matters.

Whether he is solving problems, burying them—or being guided toward them by something else entirely… is no longer a question he finds useful to ask.
